TAM-RING -- OAUTH2 CREDENTIAL MANAGER

tam-ring is a minimal OAuth2 credential manager and token broker written in
C11, depending only on the standard C libraries (POSIX libc) and libtam
(https://github.com/greenermoose/libtam).

tam-ring is designed to supply valid OAuth2 access tokens (XOAUTH2 /
OAUTHBEARER) to terminal mail and CLI tools -- such as tam-mail, mbsync
(isync), msmtp, and neomutt -- without a heavyweight language runtime
(Haskell/Rust/Python) or mandatory background daemon.

Status: Planning & initial repository setup (pre-v0.1.0). No C source files
have been written yet; see ROADMAP.md for the phased design and
implementation plan.


DESIGN PHILOSOPHY

- Pure C11 + libtam Only:
  Links statically against libtam.a (tam_str, tam_fs, tam_fs_tree,
  tam_path, tam_exec, tam_ini, tam_sha256). Fast 0-2 ms cold start and
  sub-megabyte RSS.

- No TLS/Crypto Bloat in Binary:
  Rather than linking OpenSSL, libcurl, GnuPG, or D-Bus/libsecret into
  tam-ring, HTTPS token requests and encrypted token storage are delegated
  via tam_exec to focused system tools (curl for HTTPS, and gpg, age, or
  secret-tool for encrypted persistence).

- oama-First On-Demand Architecture:
  Like oama (https://github.com/pdobsan/oama), tam-ring runs on demand when
  a client invokes "tam-ring access <account>" (e.g., via PassCmd or
  passwordeval), refreshing expired tokens transparently and exiting
  cleanly.

- Best Ideas from pizauth:
  While avoiding a mandatory resident daemon, tam-ring adopts strong
  operational ideas from pizauth (https://github.com/ltratt/pizauth),
  including configurable http_listen bindings for SSH-tunneled remote auth,
  refresh_before_expiry lead windows, auth_notify_cmd / token_event_cmd
  hooks, dump/restore pipelines, and machine-readable --json output.


PLANNED CLI INTERFACE

  tam-ring authorize <account> [--device]   Initial PKCE or Device Code flow
  tam-ring access <account>                 Print valid access token
  tam-ring renew <account>                  Force refresh of access token
  tam-ring show <account> [--json]          Inspect token metadata and expiry
  tam-ring dump                             Export token state to stdout
  tam-ring restore                          Import token state from stdin
  tam-ring printenv                         Print resolved XDG paths & config


INTEGRATION EXAMPLES (PLANNED)

  mbsync (.mbsyncrc):       PassCmd "tam-ring access user@example.com"
  msmtp (msmtprc):          passwordeval "tam-ring access user@example.com"
  tam-mail (accounts.ini):  oauth_cmd = tam-ring access user@example.com


PLANNED CONFIGURATION & PATHS

Configuration and encrypted token state follow XDG conventions via
tam_xdg_path:

  $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/tamlinux/tam-ring/tam-ring.ini
      Storage backend, refresh_before_expiry, http_listen, hooks, accounts
  $XDG_STATE_HOME/tamlinux/tam-ring/
      Encrypted per-account token files (tam_fs_lock + tam_fs_write_atomic)


BUILD PREREQUISITES (PLANNED)

- C11 compiler (gcc or clang)
- POSIX libc (glibc or musl) and GNU Make
- libtam (libtam.a)
- Runtime tools: curl (HTTPS token exchange) and age/gpg/secret-tool

See ROADMAP.md for the milestone breakdown from v0.1.0 to v1.0.0.
